#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088616} :activity/id #uuid "c4ddd229-d7db-5b1a-a201-7aaf8cc88aac" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049242 :topic/id #uuid "e60c86a6-3bce-542f-8289-5aacf26e48e4" :topic/math-academy-id 846} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088567 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088995 :tutorial/content "So, we know that the polynomial $p(x) = x^3-2x^2-x+2$ can be factored as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-x+2 & =(x-2)({x}^{2}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy dividing both sides by $(x-2),$ we can rewrite this equation as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^3-2x^2-x+2}{x-2} = x^2-1.\n$$\n\nSince the result of dividing the polynomial $p(x)$ by the factor $({x-{\\color{black}2}})$ gives *another* polynomial, namely $x^2-1,$ we say that $({x-{\\color{black}2}})$ divides $p(x)$ *with no remainder*. \n\nWe can confirm that we get a remainder of zero when $p(x)$ is divided by $({x-{\\color{blue}2}})$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{blue}2$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $2$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $2$ | $0$ | $-2$ |\n|  | $1$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $0$ |" :tutorial/id #uuid "1471f79a-59cb-50b0-b82f-f640e329bb7c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9720 :tutorial/title "Connection With Polynomial Division"} :step/id #uuid "f91395f3-3117-5229-a814-b5ebada7f90c" :step/math-academy-id 191472 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088617}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088616 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088933 :tutorial/content "If we know a *root* of a polynomial, we can immediately determine a *factor* of the polynomial using the **factor theorem**. \n\nThe factor theorem states that if $p(x)$ is a polynomial and $x={\\color{blue}{r}}$ is a *root* of $p(x),$ meaning that \n\n$$\np({\\color{blue}{r}})=0\n$$\n\nthen $(x-{\\color{blue}{r}})$ is a *factor* of $p(x).$\n\nThe converse of the factor theorem is also true. In other words, if $(x-{\\color{blue}{r}})$ is a factor of $p(x),$ then $x={\\color{blue}{r}}$ is a root of the polynomial.\n\nFor example, consider the polynomial $p(x),$ defined as\n\n$$\np(x)=x^3-2x^2-x+2.\n$$\n\nNote that $x = {\\color{blue}2}$ is a root of this polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(2) & ={2}^{3}-2(2)^{2}-2+2 \\\\  & =8-8-2+2 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, according to the factor theorem, $(x-{\\color{blue}2})$ is a *factor* of the polynomial. Indeed, it can be shown that this polynomial can be factored as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-x+2 & =(x-2)({x}^{2}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe'll learn more about factoring polynomials using the factor theorem in future lessons. But for now, we can check that this is correct by multiplying the parentheses." :tutorial/id #uuid "6ffefc48-3af9-5767-83c4-bf215db91563" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3719 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "848515fd-f525-5e59-ac3a-e5f8bb64943f" :step/math-academy-id 20941 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088567}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088617 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087755 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087571 :question/id #uuid "01993d51-8a9f-5156-b5cc-2784e93da5aa" :question/math-academy-id "e-5753"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "23c4516d-ba9c-5b55-b971-ceeb575d50c3" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048369} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051061}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088153} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088297} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522713} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522746} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537924} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537935} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537937} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559822} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559853} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559878}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Factor Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "cd1ba65e-8d34-5dd7-89fc-a3588ee3811c" :step/math-academy-id 20942 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088618}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088618 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088934 :tutorial/content "We can also apply the factor theorem to factors of the form $(ax-b).$\n\nFor example, suppose that $(2x-3)$ is a factor of a polynomial $p(x).$ Then, we can immediately obtain a root of $p(x)$ by setting the factor equal to $0$ and solving for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-3 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =3 \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the factor theorem, it follows that \n\n$$\np\\left(\\dfrac32\\right) = 0.\n$$\n\nWe can summarize this more general form of the factor theorem as follows:\n\n- If $({\\color{red}a}x-{\\color{blue}b})$ is a factor of a polynomial $p(x),$ then $x=\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}b}}{{\\color{red}a}}$ is a root of $p(x).$\n\n- Conversely, if $x=\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}b}}{{\\color{red}a}}$ is a root of $p(x),$ then $({\\color{red}a}x-{\\color{blue}b})$ is a factor of $p(x).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "cd34f044-e27c-5559-bb43-7d385325b69e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3720 :tutorial/title "The Factor Theorem for General Linear Factors"} :step/id #uuid "4e0fd1fd-b17d-5ee1-99f9-799218de5423" :step/math-academy-id 20943 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088619}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088619 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087756 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087572 :question/id #uuid "da4e2c42-894b-5f2a-917b-a5df22f41019" :question/math-academy-id "e-5754"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd5d3a7d-ef95-5d7d-a92a-ce76d6b400bb" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048673}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088157} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088298} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522720} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522791} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559806} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559864}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Factor Theorem to Factors With Leading Coefficients"} :step/id #uuid "b6addca5-a5ce-5e5a-ba37-b179ad6bd1f6" :step/math-academy-id 20944}] :activity/title "The Factor Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087917}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538013} :activity/id #uuid "d838f33a-4f86-5744-a7fc-eb75d6e74ec7" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049242 :topic/id #uuid "e60c86a6-3bce-542f-8289-5aacf26e48e4" :topic/math-academy-id 846} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538003 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537937 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ce67f95-6bea-5f1e-b9d2-1af1c79e9ab5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537288} {:answer/id #uuid "6cf05eee-7227-5199-af37-fe648f0fca6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537438} {:answer/id #uuid "db03f727-83ad-5c17-8bee-a86151b4ee85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537668} {:answer/id #uuid "e9c51bf4-bc5f-5e98-b7fe-9fe7917b1585" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537704} {:answer/id #uuid "eb45be13-00dd-5fd8-b791-31a74d07d791" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537711}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537711} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4b04c50-0203-5dfc-9b1a-8237ab5897f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537843}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "80bbf2bd-d1de-5b2e-a143-1c03752fb710" :question/math-academy-id "q-112375" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{3}-64?$\n\nI. $x=-4$ is a root of $f(x)$\n\nII. $(x+4)$ is a factor of $f(x)$\n\nIII. $(x+4)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder" :question/worked-solution "The factor theorem states that $(x-r)$ is a factor of a polynomial $p(x)$ if and only if the root $x=r$ is also a root of $p(x).$ \n\nHere, we are considering $(x+4).$ The root of this expression can be found by setting the expression equal to $0$ and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+4 & =0 \\\\ x & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now check each of the statements, in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, $x=-4$ is not a root of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-4) & =(-4)^{3}-64 \\\\  & =-64-64 \\\\  & =-128 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the factor theorem, since $x=-4$ is not a root of $f(x),$ then $(x+4)$ is not a factor of $f(x).$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $(x+4)$ is not a factor of $f(x),$ then it does not divide $f(x)$ with zero remainder.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""} :step/id #uuid "2e16a771-02ef-5ecb-9a92-67ab9fc2574e"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538007 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537935 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f287a40-81c1-5aaa-a86b-d2b035d996fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537254} {:answer/id #uuid "613837ad-89d1-5321-957d-108904a65b60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537421} {:answer/id #uuid "71f3178c-d1ea-5a2a-a611-b33edaa1e845" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537452} {:answer/id #uuid "730eb180-4edc-5379-bca0-fecd47808404" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537455} {:answer/id #uuid "d3344a98-2866-5a55-94ff-6269de66ca21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537644}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537421} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9167685-de61-5642-a6f3-e2034b8507d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537868}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7c34a96f-d461-5b11-9cb5-80d7557e52d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-73777" :question/problem "For which of the following polynomials is $(x+1)$ a factor?\n\nI. $f(x)={x}^{2}+3x+2$\n\nII. $g(x)={x}^{2}+8x+7$\n\nIII. $h(x)=-{x}^{2}+x$" :question/worked-solution "The factor theorem states that $(x-r)$ is a factor of a polynomial $p(x)$ if and only if the root $x=r$ is also a root of $p(x).$ \n\nHere, we are considering $(x+1).$ The root of this expression can be found by setting the expression equal to $0$ and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & =0 \\\\ x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the factor theorem, $(x+1)$ is a factor of $p(x)$ if the root $x=-1$ is a root of $p(x).$ \n\nSo now, we will check if $x=-1$ is a root of the polynomials $f,$ $g$ and $h.$\n\n- Evaluating $x=-1$ at $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =(-1)^{2}+3(-1)+2 \\\\  & =1-3+2 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, by the factor theorem, we conclude that $(x+1)$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$\n\n- Now, evaluating $x=-1$ at $g(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-1) & =(-1)^{2}+8(-1)+7 \\\\  & =1-8+7 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, by the factor theorem, we conclude that $(x+1)$ is indeed a factor of $g(x).$\n\n- Finally, evaluating $x=-1$ at $h(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-1) & =-(-1)^{2}+(-1) \\\\  & =-1-1 \\\\  & =-2\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "3825af64-8557-55d7-8fbb-b760b9c6080d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538003}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538013 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537924 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "002539d9-f83f-5f8a-821f-f91ec24993f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537223} {:answer/id #uuid "17a8904d-7494-54a8-9cf6-42abe4eadb2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537279} {:answer/id #uuid "6fc2e5c4-3531-5d25-8436-a4eb1e7ccd7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537445} {:answer/id #uuid "b074b252-d948-500c-8a9b-b0d2c27004f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537567} {:answer/id #uuid "f2645d9f-4118-565c-be24-6f9684808038" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537729}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537567} :answer-field/id #uuid "63b70b6a-3c22-5376-9d31-4b93eb63027c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537794}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6b8e59d5-6b05-50ce-8455-191b0c2a0b7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-73776" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{2}+5x+4?$\n\nI. $x=4$ is a root of $f(x)$\n\nII. $(x-4)$ is a factor of $f(x)$\n\nIII. $(x-4)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder" :question/worked-solution "The factor theorem states that $(x-r)$ is a factor of a polynomial $p(x)$ if and only if the root $x=r$ is also a root of $p(x).$ \n\nHere, we are considering $(x-4).$ The root of this expression can be found by setting the expression equal to $0$ and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-4 & =0 \\\\ x & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now check each of the statements, in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, $x=4$ is not a root of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(4) & ={4}^{2}+5(4)+4 \\\\  & =16+20+4 \\\\  & =40 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the factor theorem, since $x=4$ is not a root of $f(x),$ then $(x-4)$ is not a factor of $f(x).$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $(x-4)$ is not a factor of $f(x),$ then it does not divide $f(x)$ with zero remainder.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\""} :step/id #uuid "43326e46-5e1e-5d89-9515-b93fb570546c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538007}}] :activity/title "The Factor Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537218}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559924} :activity/id #uuid "17431833-f890-5a9b-bd60-79825d278655" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049242 :topic/id #uuid "e60c86a6-3bce-542f-8289-5aacf26e48e4" :topic/math-academy-id 846} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559896 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559878 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2c79867-73d6-5cc0-a3af-25cf7ccb5cd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559495} {:answer/id #uuid "c845ff5b-3e9e-5056-902c-a2214e1aa90b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559503} {:answer/id #uuid "d069d041-5456-57d8-b6c8-da7c7f8df610" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559520} {:answer/id #uuid "f02ef9f3-2866-5904-be26-7b7de039fb1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559599} {:answer/id #uuid "fce13d87-4fc4-577a-a664-6cc3884100f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559637}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559599} :answer-field/id #uuid "c70a0b59-1c4a-5aa7-bb33-d1a6a216cf95" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559745}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e986899c-1ec0-569d-94ba-d6811b53879c" :question/math-academy-id "q-73779" :question/problem "For which of the following polynomials is $(x-2)$ a factor?\n\nI. $f(x)={x}^{3}-4x$\n\nII. $g(x)={x}^{2}+4x+5$\n\nIII. $h(x)={x}^{2}-4x+4$" :question/worked-solution "The factor theorem states that $(x-r)$ is a factor of a polynomial $p(x)$ if and only if the root $x=r$ is also a root of $p(x).$ \n\nHere, we are considering $(x-2).$ The root of this expression can be found by setting the expression equal to $0$ and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2 & =0 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the factor theorem, $(x-2)$ is a factor of $p(x)$ if the root $x=2$ is a root of $p(x).$ \n\nSo now, we will check if $x=2$ is a root of the polynomials $f,$ $g$ and $h.$\n\n- Evaluating $x=2$ at $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2) & ={2}^{3}-4(2) \\\\  & =8-8 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, by the factor theorem, we conclude that $(x-2)$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$\n\n- Now, evaluating $x=2$ at $g(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(2) & ={2}^{2}+4(2)+5 \\\\  & =4+8+5 \\\\  & =17\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by the factor theorem, $(x-2)$ is not a factor of $g(x).$\n\n- Finally, evaluating $x=2$ at $h(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(2) & ={2}^{2}-4(2)+4 \\\\  & =4-8+4 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only .\""} :step/id #uuid "05656f4c-c5e5-5079-9eb0-b865d0bab77a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559912}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559912 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559806 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "016050f4-b327-51eb-ac59-6cdb4f2d394b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559017} {:answer/id #uuid "1649ec6b-8d70-5b6e-8662-99e140405802" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559064} {:answer/id #uuid "80f25a70-a063-50e7-b4ec-df4df125ef08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559338} {:answer/id #uuid "c1a088d6-83f2-526a-b6ad-dd5f6c749f89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559492} {:answer/id #uuid "cc39eb67-2c40-56a3-bfba-b0087f7d23c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559511}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559338} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e9c46f9-73fa-5bae-8ba5-d402d0aa2e48" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559705}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "50aaea5b-0826-518a-8a27-2b0395848ac8" :question/math-academy-id "q-73789" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the polynomial $f(x)=3{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-3x-2?$\n\nI. $x=-\\frac{2}{3}$ is a root of $f(x)$\n\nII. $(3x+2)$ is a factor of $f(x)$\n\nIII. $(3x+2)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder" :question/worked-solution "The factor theorem states that $(ax-b)$ is a factor of the polynomial $p(x)$ if and only if the root $x=\\frac{b}{a}$ is also root of $p(x).$ \n\nHere, we are considering $(3x+2).$ The root of this expression can be found by setting the expression equal to $0$ and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+2 & =0 \\\\ 3x & =-2 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check each of the statements, in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $x=-\\frac{2}{3}$ is a root of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-\\frac{2}{3}) & =3{(-\\frac{2}{3})}^{3}+2{(-\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}-3(-\\frac{2}{3})-2 \\\\  & =3(-\\frac{8}{27})+2(\\frac{4}{9})+3(\\frac{2}{3})-2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{9}+\\frac{8}{9}+2-2 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. According to the factor theorem, since $x=-\\frac{2}{3}$ is a root of $f(x),$ then $(3x+2)$ is a factor of $f(x).$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $(3x+2)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ then $(3x+2)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} :step/id #uuid "2c87655d-7944-5ac9-9c29-bd3ab5f7338f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559978}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559924 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559822 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35e82de2-f5a4-5a17-8312-4f73519d2e6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559150} {:answer/id #uuid "47ca42e7-e9b5-518e-9713-80c7cee79696" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559203} {:answer/id #uuid "9e82d34b-fe77-5fe5-9e29-97290fc018c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559418} {:answer/id #uuid "c527e597-42e6-5d49-9d63-cfb389529772" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559499} {:answer/id #uuid "fec4a528-b0f6-5c07-bfc6-11532012aef8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559640}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559150} :answer-field/id #uuid "f84d9338-e966-5479-8801-6e8f15658f84" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559768}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "81cebe4e-1d65-5357-b789-c0fc16951ab5" :question/math-academy-id "q-38040" :question/problem "For which of the following polynomials is $(x+3)$ a factor?\n\nI. $f(x)=2{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-8x+3$\n\nII. $g(x)={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-x-3$\n\nIII. $h(x)={x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-x-2$" :question/worked-solution "The factor theorem states that $(x-r)$ is a factor of a polynomial $p(x)$ if and only if the root $x=r$ is also a root of $p(x).$ \n\nHere, we are considering $(x+3).$ The root of this expression can be found by setting the expression equal to $0$ and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+3 & =0 \\\\ x & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the factor theorem, $(x+3)$ is a factor of $p(x)$ if the root $x=-3$ is a root of $p(x).$ \n\nSo now, we will check if $x=-3$ is a root of the polynomials $f,$ $g$ and $h.$\n\n- Evaluating $x=-3$ at $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-3) & =2(-3)^{3}+3(-3)^{2}-8(-3)+3 \\\\  & =2(-27)+3(9)+24+3 \\\\  & =-54+27+24+3 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, by the factor theorem, we conclude that $(x+3)$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$\n\n- Now, evaluating $x=-3$ at $g(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-3) & =(-3)^{3}+3(-3)^{2}-(-3)-3 \\\\  & =-27+3(9)+3-3 \\\\  & =-27+27+3-3 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, by the factor theorem, we conclude that $(x+3)$ is indeed a factor of $g(x).$\n\n- Finally, evaluating $x=-3$ at $h(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-3) & =(-3)^{3}+2(-3)^{2}-(-3)-2 \\\\  & =-27+2(9)+3-2 \\\\  & =-27+18+3-2 \\\\  & =-8\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "493d11f3-2102-562a-b794-a790e09ff1be" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559896}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559949 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559864 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50a01771-cde1-54b6-903b-5be07bb31b68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559222} {:answer/id #uuid "7215867e-586e-537b-aa3c-c7a87d387e5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559298} {:answer/id #uuid "7b90dcb3-20f2-5889-885c-583181c9f4e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559323} {:answer/id #uuid "a0014fe0-63da-51e9-bf6d-ad45b394652b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559421} {:answer/id #uuid "fedcbdd6-f4a6-5750-a08c-d1f7b5c28221" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559641}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559298} :answer-field/id #uuid "8508e80e-2237-5e61-952e-da76d1a6dd3a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559719}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cef78ed2-9af9-556a-8a9d-05a7101331ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-112468" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{3}-3x+2?$\n\nI. $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ is a root of $f(x)$\n\nII. $(2x+1)$ is a factor of $f(x)$\n\nIII. $(2x+1)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder" :question/worked-solution "The factor theorem states that $(ax-b)$ is a factor of the polynomial $p(x)$ if and only if the root $x=\\frac{b}{a}$ is also root of $p(x).$ \n\nHere, we are considering $(2x+1).$ The root of this expression can be found by setting the expression equal to $0$ and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+1 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =-1 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check each of the statements, in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ is not a root of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-\\frac{1}{2}) & ={(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{3}-3(-\\frac{1}{2})+2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{3}{2}+2 \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{8} \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the factor theorem, since $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ is not a root of $f(x),$ then $(2x+1)$ is not a factor of $f(x).$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $(2x+1)$ is not a factor of $f(x),$ then it does not divide $f(x)$ with zero remainder.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""} :step/id #uuid "89659a65-f3c8-5528-aa9b-c7187752ecec"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559978 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559853 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e583ae8-d9fe-52a9-b929-554bdbe54145" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559091} {:answer/id #uuid "2e2007b5-5f0d-5777-a12b-059ac6926d9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559133} {:answer/id #uuid "9f06c2d4-90c4-5f39-a2ec-6d108a9826a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559419} {:answer/id #uuid "a94df82a-733b-5f27-86b0-983a28681f0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559441} {:answer/id #uuid "e8cb940e-d44e-5a8f-988a-50c419452c79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559584}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559584} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4b18df5-7e14-5ba9-ad19-fa824c06c4c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559751}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c04c2ae6-0944-57e5-bf4e-7441e7fccca7" :question/math-academy-id "q-112376" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{2}+x-6?$\n\nI. $x=3$ is a root of $f(x)$\n\nII. $(x-3)$ is a factor of $f(x)$\n\nIII. $(x-3)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder" :question/worked-solution "The factor theorem states that $(x-r)$ is a factor of a polynomial $p(x)$ if and only if the root $x=r$ is also a root of $p(x).$ \n\nHere, we are considering $(x-3).$ The root of this expression can be found by setting the expression equal to $0$ and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-3 & =0 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now check each of the statements, in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, $x=3$ is not a root of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3) & ={3}^{2}+(3)-6 \\\\  & =9+3-6 \\\\  & =6 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the factor theorem, since $x=3$ is not a root of $f(x),$ then $(x-3)$ is not a factor of $f(x).$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $(x-3)$ is not a factor of $f(x),$ then it does not divide $f(x)$ with zero remainder.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""} :step/id #uuid "e5a159e3-2dc9-58e6-b01c-29cc364c394c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559949}}] :activity/title "The Factor Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558996}]}
